================================================================================
Sotto — in-browser tutor STT/LLM-contention regression: root cause + fix
Run: 2026-09-05, macOS 15 / M-series, Chromium via Playwright, headless,
     --enable-unsafe-webgpu --use-angle=metal.

BACKGROUND
  planning/BROWSER-TUTOR.md's "Slice 2 + 3 status" note flagged, unproven:
  whisper transcription of a 2.7s clip took 1.05s and was correct in the
  slice-1 e2e (STT only, no LLM), but ~21s and garbage ("de de de de...")
  in two slice-2/3 runs, both with the ~1.1GB WebLLM Qwen3 engine also
  resident on the same WebGPU device. Leading hypothesis: WebGPU resource
  contention between the two models. This log disproves that hypothesis,
  identifies the real cause, and records the fix.

METHOD
  Diagnostic-only hooks were added (never exercised by a real session):
  `WorkerInitPayload.debug` (protocol.ts) with `skipLlm` and
  `forceSttDevice`, threaded through provider.ts and read by
  sessionManager.ts from `window.__SOTTO_TUTOR_DEBUG__` (a global the real
  app never sets). browser-tutor.mjs gained env vars to drive it:
  DEBUG_SKIP_LLM, DEBUG_STT_DEVICE, STT_ONLY (stop right after the first
  `stt_ms` metric instead of waiting out the full two-turn flow),
  SINGLE_UTTERANCE (restore slice-1's one-utterance fixture instead of the
  two-utterance tool-round-trip wav), PROFILE_NAME (name the persistent
  Chromium profile, so a "fresh profile" run is trivial to get).

  Ground truth for the audio itself: the exact synthesized fixture wav was
  also fed to this machine's native whisper-server (ggml-large-v3-turbo,
  127.0.0.1:9001) directly, bypassing the browser pipeline entirely.

EXPERIMENT TABLE

  (a) LLM never loaded (debug.skipLlm), llama-server RUNNING (the state
      found at the start of this session), reused profile.
        stt_load_ms = 2382ms webgpu
        stt_ms      = 21042ms webgpu  ->  "de la de de de de de de de de de..."
      SLOW AND WRONG, with the LLM not resident in this worker at all.
      Already inconsistent with "WebLLM residency causes it."

  (c) LLM never loaded, llama-server STOPPED (confirmed via
      `curl :8790/health` -> llm:false and `pgrep llama-server` empty
      before and after), fresh Chromium profile (never loaded this model
      before, so no stale cache).
        stt_load_ms = 1668-2062ms webgpu
        stt_ms      = 20791-21809ms webgpu  ->  "de de de de de de..."
      SLOW AND WRONG, with the LLM not resident ANYWHERE in the browser
      process and the only other GPU consumer on the machine stopped.
      Rules out both native-process GPU contention and WebLLM residency.

  (extra) Same as (c) but with the fake-mic wav trimmed to a SINGLE
      utterance (SINGLE_UTTERANCE=1) — slice-1's exact fixture shape,
      ruling out "the two-utterance tool-round-trip wav changes VAD
      segmentation of the first utterance."
        stt_ms = 20492ms webgpu  ->  "que a que que que que que que..."
      Still slow and wrong. Rules out the audio-fixture-shape hypothesis.

  (extra) Ground truth: the identical raw wav segment, submitted directly
      to the native whisper-server (large-v3-turbo, no browser involved):
        -> "¿Qué significa la palabra cigarra?"  (correct)
      The audio is fine. The defect is inside the browser STT call.

  (d) STT forced onto wasm (DEBUG_STT_DEVICE=wasm) with the ORIGINAL
      catalog dtype (encoder fp16, decoder_model_merged q8):
        stt_load_failed = "Can't create a session. ERROR_CODE: 1,
          ... InsertedPrecisionFreeCast_/layer_norm/... " (encoder)
        stt_load_failed = "... qdq_actions.cc:137 TransposeDQWeightsFor
          MatMulNBits Missing required scale: ...decoder.embed_tokens...
          " (decoder, once the encoder's dtype was patched to fp32)
      wasm's CPU execution provider cannot even BUILD a session from
      either the fp16 encoder or the q8 decoder_model_merged export —
      a hard graph-build failure, not the silent upcast models.ts's
      original comment assumed. This means the documented fallback-matrix
      claim ("adapter load fails -> worker retries on wasm automatically")
      was never actually exercised for this model/dtype combination.

  (e) `adapter.limits` (requested every run once WebGPU is confirmed
      present): nothing resembling a degraded/memory-limited adapter —
      maxBufferSize and maxStorageBufferBindingSize both report the
      normal 4294967292 (~4GB-1) ceiling, texture dimensions at the
      standard 16384/2048/2048. No sign of a fallback or software adapter.

ROOT CAUSE
  The fp16 ENCODER dtype (models.ts's original `encoder_model: 'fp16'`)
  is what breaks WebGPU decoding on this environment. Isolated two ways:
    1. Generation-kwargs experiment: adding `max_new_tokens: 128` and
       `no_repeat_ngram_size: 3` to the STT call (previously nothing was
       passed at all) turned the failure from "slow AND garbage" into
       "fast but still wrong" (695ms, "que no"; or 618-827ms, empty) —
       proving the ~20s cost IS the decoder running to its token budget
       in a repetition loop, not the "same slow work" contention would
       predict. This is a symptom-mask, not a fix (still wrong).
    2. Targeted A/B, the decisive test: WebGPU device, encoder forced to
       fp32, decoder LEFT AT q8 (no device change, no download-size
       explosion) — repeated on two independent fresh profiles:
         stt_ms = 1357ms webgpu -> "¿Qué significa la palabra cigarrara?"
         stt_ms = 1023ms webgpu -> "significa la palabra cigarrera."
       Correct (modulo whisper-base's own known ending/first-word
       imprecision — the SAME "cigarrara" typo the slice-1 log already
       documented as "whisper-base's ending, not a bug"), and fast.
  Conclusion: the fp16 encoder export triggers a WebGPU-side decoder
  repetition-collapse in the currently pinned @huggingface/transformers /
  onnxruntime-web versions on this hardware. It has nothing to do with
  the LLM, with the native llama-server process, with the browser
  profile, or with the audio. The original hypothesis in
  planning/BROWSER-TUTOR.md is DISPROVEN.

FIX SHIPPED
  1. models.ts: `STT_MODEL.dtype.encoder_model` changed fp16 -> fp32.
     Decoder stays q8 (still correct per the original accuracy
     measurement in this same file; q8 only costs punctuation/casing).
     `sizeMb` updated 95 -> 136 (fp32 encoder is larger; still far under
     whisper-small's ~249MB q8 export that was ruled out for budget).
  2. worker.ts's `dtypeForDevice`: since wasm cannot build a session from
     the q8 decoder at all (experiment (d)), the wasm path additionally
     upgrades the decoder to fp32 — used only when the wasm attempt is
     actually taken (the encoder is already fp32 in the catalog for
     every device, so no per-device encoder override is needed anymore).
  3. Defense in depth, independent of the encoder fix: the STT call now
     passes `no_repeat_ngram_size: 3, max_new_tokens: 128` so any future
     decoder-collapse (this model, or a future model swap) fails FAST
     and bounded instead of silently expensive.
  4. `packages/voice/src/browser-cascade/stt-fallback.ts` (new):
     `isDegenerateTranscript` (repeated-token detector) and
     `SttFallbackTracker` (session-scoped: a slow (>8s) or degenerate
     WebGPU attempt permanently switches that session's STT to wasm,
     discards the bad transcript instead of feeding it to the LLM, and
     emits a one-time tutor-caption note). Pure logic, no ML calls — unit
     tested with fake timings/transcripts in
     packages/voice/test/browser-cascade-stt-fallback.test.ts (13 tests).
     This is the safety net the task asked for; with the encoder fix in
     place it is not expected to trigger in normal operation, but it now
     also guards the case where the wasm fallback itself needs a
     different dtype than whatever is already cached for another device
     (see CAVEAT below).

VERIFICATION (post-fix, fresh profile, llama-server confirmed stopped,
  LLM not loaded — isolating STT exactly as in the failing experiments
  above):
    stt_load_ms = 5019ms webgpu   (fp32 encoder is bigger; one-time, at
                                    session connect, not per-turn)
    stt_ms      = 1023ms webgpu   2.5s segment
    caption: "significa la palabra cigarrera."
  Correct, fast, no fallback triggered. Well under both the 8s fallback
  threshold and the 5s per-turn target.

CAVEAT — reactive mid-session fallback and cache granularity
  transformers.js's `experimental_transformers-hash-cache` appears to key
  on model id, not on model id + dtype: reusing an ALREADY-webgpu-loaded
  profile and then forcing a wasm reload with a different dtype
  (SttFallbackTracker's actual runtime behavior) sometimes reused stale
  cached files and hit the same "Can't create a session" error as
  experiment (d), even after the dtype fix, on a profile that had
  already loaded the model once under a different dtype. This did NOT
  reproduce on a genuinely fresh profile (a real first-time visitor).
  Given the encoder fix removes the need for the reactive fallback to
  fire in the first place, this is recorded as a known limitation of the
  safety net rather than chased further: if it ever engages on a
  returning visitor whose profile already cached the webgpu dtype files,
  the wasm reload may itself fail, at which point `stt_fallback_reload_failed`
  is logged and the session continues caption-only for STT until the
  learner reloads the page (a fresh worker re-derives from the same
  profile's cache, so this is a rare, one-time rough edge, not a stuck
  state).

WHAT THIS DOES NOT EXPLAIN
  The full slice-4 e2e run (docs/evidence/browser-tutor-slice4-2026-09-05.log)
  surfaced a separate, pre-existing issue unrelated to STT: the tutor's
  first LLM turn sometimes completes with no visible reply at all (state
  goes thinking -> listening with no caption, no error, no metric). The
  same silent-first-turn shape is visible in the original
  docs/evidence/browser-tutor-slice2-3-2026-09-05.log's turn 1, so this
  predates every change in this log and is out of scope for the STT
  regression this document is about. Not investigated further here.
================================================================================
